---
name: std-os-readline
version: 1.0.0
backends: [int, v2]
---

# `std.os.readLine` reads a line, and says `None` at end of input

Reported as [#76](https://github.com/sergey-scherbina/scalascript/issues/76): a program could
print a prompt and then had no way to read the answer. `readLine` was unbound on every lane, and
`std/os.ssc` exported the whole environment surface with nothing that reads input — so an
interactive `.ssc` program (a REPL, a confirmation prompt, a wizard) was not expressible at all.

**The gate is driven by a here-string, not by a terminal.** The conformance runner feeds every case
an empty stdin, which is precisely the EOF case — so the first `readLine()` here returns `None` and
that is what the golden records. A case that needed someone to type would not be a gate; this one
tests the branch that a terminal never shows you and that every caller has to handle.

**`backends: [int, v2]` is a measurement, not a shortcut.** `std/os` resolves on the interpreter,
the native tier and the v2 route, and does NOT resolve on `js` or `jvm` — measured on
`envOrElse`, which fails there with `not callable: ()` and `value envOrElse is not a member of
object std.os` respectively. That is a gap in the whole `std/os` surface rather than in this
primitive, filed separately; gating `readLine` on lanes where `env` does not work either would be
testing the wrong thing.

```scalascript
[readLine](std/os.ssc)
```

```scalascript
def describe(line: Option[String]): String =
  line match
    case Some(text) => "line: " + text
    case None       => "eof"

def main() =
  // Empty stdin: the first read is already end-of-input.
  println(describe(readLine()))
  // And it stays None — a caller that loops must terminate, not spin.
  println(describe(readLine()))
```
